<Base
  title="Can an ML potential reproduce a switchable catalyst? · Aryan Agarwal"
  description="Benchmarking Meta FAIR's UMA machine-learning interatomic potential against DFT for hydrogen binding on a polarization-switchable SrRuO3/BaTiO3 catalyst."
>
  <article class="article">
    <a class="back" href="/">← Home</a>
    <p class="mono muted" style="margin:0;font-size:.85rem">Research · ATLAS Materials Physics Lab, UC San Diego · Oct 2026 – present</p>
    <h1>Can a machine-learning potential reproduce a switchable catalyst?</h1>
    <p class="lede">I benchmarked Meta FAIR's UMA foundation potential against density functional theory (DFT) for hydrogen binding on a ferroelectric catalyst whose behavior flips with its polarization.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <p><strong>In one paragraph:</strong> a thin SrRuO<sub>3</sub> catalyst on ferroelectric BaTiO<sub>3</sub> should bind hydrogen strongly in one polarization state and weakly in the other. Lab DFT puts the gap between the two states at <strong>0.91 eV</strong>. Running on the lab's own DFT structures, an off-the-shelf UMA potential gets the direction right with every oxide-trained head (one state binds H, the other releases it). It recovers <strong>0.66 eV</strong> with the like-for-like head and up to <strong>0.77 eV</strong> with the closest one, about 70–85% of the gap, in minutes on a laptop GPU.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="tiles">
      <li><span class="v">0.66–0.77 eV</span><span class="l">ML switching gap (like-for-like head → closest head) vs 0.91 eV from DFT</span></li>
      <li><span class="v">0.19 s</span><span class="l">per UMA energy + forces call (62 atoms, laptop GPU), vs ~2.6 min per single DFT SCF iteration on the Expanse cluster (a DFT energy needs many)</span></li>
      <li><span class="v">~17 min</span><span class="l">whole comparison on a laptop GPU</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why this matters</h2>
    <p>A good catalyst has to hold reaction intermediates "just right": tightly enough to react, loosely enough to let go. That trade-off (the Sabatier principle) caps how fast a static catalyst can be. <em>Dynamic</em> catalysis tries to get around it by switching the surface between a "grab" state and a "release" state.</p>
    <p>Ferroelectrics like BaTiO<sub>3</sub> are a natural switch. A voltage moves the Ti ion slightly up or down inside its oxygen cage, which flips the material's built-in electric field. DFT work in our lab (Abbasi, 2023) predicts that this flip changes how strongly a SrRuO<sub>3</sub> skin binds hydrogen. DFT is accurate but slow, so screening many candidate materials needs a faster model. Foundation machine-learning interatomic potentials (MLIPs) are the obvious candidate. The question is whether they capture an effect this subtle.</p>

    <figure>
      <div class="frame paper">
        <Image src={polarization} alt="Diagram of a BaTiO3 unit cell in three states: no polarization with Ti centered, polarization up with Ti shifted toward the surface, and polarization down with Ti shifted into the film." widths={[680, 1360]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 696px" />
      </div>
      <figcaption><strong>The switch is a tiny shift.</strong> Moving Ti by about 0.17 Å flips the built-in field, and with it the surface chemistry. Green Ba, red O, grey Ti.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>What I found</h2>
    <figure>
      <div class="frame">
        <ThemedFigure name="dg" alt="Dot plot of hydrogen binding free energy for six setups. In every setup polarization-up is below zero (−0.28 to −0.51 eV) and polarization-down is above zero (+0.26 to +0.40 eV)." width={640} height={460} />
      </div>
      <figcaption><strong>Every setup reproduces the switch:</strong> polarization-up binds H (ΔG &lt; 0) and polarization-down releases it (ΔG &gt; 0). H is placed atop Ru on the lab's DFT slabs. UMA values use ΔG = ΔE + 0.24 eV. Thesis values are as reported, under the thesis's own correction convention, so compare the gaps rather than exact positions.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure>
      <div class="frame">
        <ThemedFigure name="ddg" alt="Bar chart of the switching gap ΔΔG by model head: oc25 0.77, omat 0.66, oc22 0.62, omat plus D3 0.60, oc22 plus D3 0.54, and the first hand-built slab 0.41 eV. A vertical line marks the thesis DFT value of 0.91 eV." width={640} height={440} />
      </div>
      <figcaption><strong>Across all five setups UMA recovers about 60–85% of the DFT gap, and the structure mattered most.</strong> ΔΔG = ΔG(down) − ΔG(up) at the same Ru site. The hydrogen reference and the free-energy constant cancel in it, which makes it the fairest number to compare across codes. My first hand-built slab topped out at 0.41 eV. The lab's DFT structures (different termination, cell and constraints) closed most of the gap.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>What I built</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A <strong>fault-tolerant, resumable GPU pipeline</strong> (PyTorch, CUDA, ASE, pymatgen) that places and relaxes adsorbates with Meta's AdsorbML, and also runs forced-site calculations, across model heads and dispersion settings.</li>
      <li>A <strong>benchmark across 4 UMA heads</strong> (OC20, OC22, OC25, OMat) with and without DFT-D3 dispersion, on UMA 1.2 and 1.2.1. The OC20 head (trained on metals, no oxides) predicted the switch backwards on my first rebuilt slab, so I dropped it for this system.</li>
      <li><strong>Sanity checks against DFT before trusting adsorption numbers</strong>: does each head reproduce the bare-slab energy difference and geometry? (See the table below.)</li>
      <li>An <strong>audit pass</strong> that recomputes every reported number from the raw outputs. It caught a free-energy convention mismatch (+0.14 vs the standard +0.24 eV) between scripts.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Caveats I'd flag to a reviewer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Which head to trust:</strong> oc25 gives the closest gap, but it is trained on RPBE+D3 data, while the thesis used PBE+U. The like-for-like head is <strong>omat (PBE), at 0.66 eV</strong>. I treat the 0.62–0.77 eV spread across the no-D3 heads as model uncertainty.</li>
      <li><strong>Adding D3 makes the match worse</strong> here, because the reference DFT used no dispersion correction.</li>
      <li><strong>Site preference:</strong> an unconstrained AdsorbML search prefers surface O over Ru in every case, while the Ru-site story assumes H sits on Ru. That is an open question for the next step.</li>
      <li><strong>Remaining gap</strong> (0.66–0.77 vs 0.91 eV): possible causes are the H* geometry, how +U is applied (the OMat training data applies no U to Ru or Ti; the thesis does), the electrostatic boundary conditions, or plain model error.</li>
    </ul>

    <details>
      <summary>Expert detail: levels of theory, checks, full numbers</summary>
      <p><strong>Reference DFT</strong> (Quantum ESPRESSO, from the thesis): PBE+U (U = 2.5 eV Ru, 4 eV Ti), GBRV ultrasoft pseudopotentials, 40/320 Ry cutoffs, 2×4×1 k-points, no spin, no vdW, 2D Coulomb cutoff. 2×1 cell, 62 atoms, ½ ML H on Ru.</p>
      <p><strong>ML model:</strong> UMA-S 1.2.1 (fairchem). Its heads are trained on VASP/PAW data, so only energy <em>differences</em> are compared, never absolute energies. Free energy: ΔG = ΔE + ΔZPE − TΔS ≈ ΔE + 0.24 eV (Nørskov et al., 2005).</p>
